# .env.defaults — committed, non-secret defaults for the local dev recipes
# (`just tui-dev-up`, `just demo-up`). Loaded first; a gitignored `.env`
# beside it is loaded second and wins, so personal overrides and anything
# secret (e.g. GAUNTLET_SESSION_TOKEN) go there, never here.
#
# This is NOT `.env.example`: that file is the pinned, exact mirror of spec
# Annex E (the boundary suite checks it) and documents the core's env
# surface with deliberately empty values. This file holds working values.
#
# Every line uses `${VAR:-default}`, so a value already in your shell always
# wins over this file. DEV_REPO_ROOT is set by the loader before this
# file is read.

# --- Control loop (REQUIRED by `gauntlet run`, fail closed, env-only) ------
# GAUNTLET.md front matter rejects a `control:` section, so these can only
# come from the environment. Values sized for a real agent, not the mock.
GAUNTLET_STALL_TIMEOUT_MS=${GAUNTLET_STALL_TIMEOUT_MS:-600000}
GAUNTLET_RETRY_BASE_MS=${GAUNTLET_RETRY_BASE_MS:-1000}
GAUNTLET_RETRY_MAX_MS=${GAUNTLET_RETRY_MAX_MS:-30000}
GAUNTLET_MAX_ATTEMPTS_PER_UNIT=${GAUNTLET_MAX_ATTEMPTS_PER_UNIT:-2}

# --- Run state location ----------------------------------------------------
# Gauntlet's own default is the OS temp dir (/tmp/gauntlet), which a WSL
# restart wipes — taking a run's state, workspaces and evidence with it. The
# per-project recipes set GAUNTLET_STATE_DIR / GAUNTLET_ARTIFACT_DIR to an
# ABSOLUTE path under this root (the workspace provider refuses any path with
# a `..` component, so these must never be relative).
DEV_RUNS_ROOT=${DEV_RUNS_ROOT:-$DEV_REPO_ROOT/targets/.runs}

# --- Workbench (TUI) -------------------------------------------------------
# project_root and gauntlet_binary are auto-discovered (walk up for
# GAUNTLET.md; `gauntlet` on PATH, else this checkout's debug build). Set
# them only to override discovery. GAUNTLET_WORKBENCH_PRIME_BINARY is found
# on PATH by the recipes. Nothing here is set to an EMPTY value on purpose:
# the core treats set-but-empty as an explicit (often fail-closed) setting.
